{
  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to people who are usually invisible. Bakers lift warm trays from ovens; nurses step into pale streets after long shifts; cleaners rinse yesterday from glass and stone. Even the traffic lights perform for empty intersections, changing colors with the patience of machines that expect an audience.\n\nI like this hour because it makes familiar places feel unfinished. A bus shelter becomes a small theater of reflections. Pigeons inspect the pavement like serious officials. Somewhere, a radio plays behind an open window, and one song briefly connects strangers who will never meet.\n\nMorning also reveals how much ordinary life depends on quiet acts of preparation. Coffee must be brewed before conversation can begin. Floors are swept before footsteps arrive. Trains are checked before commuters complain that they are late. The world seems spontaneous only because someone has already tightened a bolt, folded a napkin, answered a message, or turned on the lights.\n\nBy midmorning, urgency returns. Doors open, phones ring, and schedules harden around us. Yet the earlier stillness leaves a residue, a reminder that every crowded day began as open space.\n\nPerhaps that is why beginnings matter. They do not promise perfection; they simply offer room. A blank page, a cleared table, an apology, or a first step can change the direction of a day. We rarely notice the moment possibility becomes routine. But each dawn rehearses the lesson: life is built quietly, before it is lived loudly, and attention is a form of gratitude.",
